  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1035 AM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nConvective complex from last night has helped to regenerate a\nclosed mid-level low embedded in the inverted trough that has been\nslowly transiting the area over the past several days. The\ninverted trough axis will lift into the Gila Region by this\nafternoon, while the upper low slowly crawls towards the Arizona\nstate line. Significant convective debris (cloud cover and light\nshowers) caught under the low will limit instability today over SW\nNew Mexico. GFS 200 mb streamlines show upper level convergence\naxis currently along the Rio Grande shifting west this afternoon\nas well, further inhibiting convective potential. Expect today\nwill mostly be a \"down\" day, especially compared to the last three\ndays. Best chances for storms will be in northern Grant County\nalong the trough axis, with isolated storms over the Sacramentos\nand Hudspeth County/the El Paso Lower Valley. A isolated storm \nunder the upper low in SW New Mexico can't be ruled out either,\nthough coverage will be limited. But anything that manages to form\nthere will have very slow storm motion. East of the Rio Grande,\nstorms will tend to train with mid and upper-level steering flow\nall from the SSE to NNW. \n\nHRRR has been consistently suggesting additional showers/storms\nwill roll up into the Lower Valley towards El Paso later tonight,\naided by a vort lobe on the back side of the departing upper low. \n\nGoing forward, a sharp downturn in precip chances is expected as\nthe subtropical ridge builds westward, boosting mid level\ntemperatures (-2 to -3C at 500 mb). Isolated storms in the\nBootheel Region, Sacramento Mountains, and Hudspeth County will be\nthe main focus for isolated storms. \n\nBy Thursday and Friday, the subtropical ridge will park over us,\nand strengthen to 598-599 dm. A notable inverted trough will move\ninto northeastern Mexico, but will pass far to our south over\nDurango Friday, reaching Baja Sur by Saturday. A rather mid-late\nJune-like pattern. Max Temps will follow suit, climbing to around\n100-105 in the lowlands late in the week, with Heat Advisories\npossibly being needed for El Paso and the Lower Valley.\n\nSome upper level moisture will sneak in from the east on Saturday,\nwhich if you trace it back, is partially recycled moisture from\ntoday working all the way around the subtropical ridge, which will\nhave drifted closer to the Four Corners by the weekend. Thus,\nexpect an uptick in precip chances, especially in the Sacs/Gila\nfor Saturday and Sunday, hopefully taking the edge off the heat.",
